Subject: Time to event analysis in DO mice
Message-ID: <RRlIT_KXj5o.v8xPUP3VCQAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl2-disc/c/RRlIT_KXj5o/m/v8xPUP3VCQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

We would really like to use a time to event as our phenotype for GWAS of DO mice, but would have some mice that will not have the event. I can enter last day of the experiment for my phenotype, but was wondering if there was a different way to do this that incorporates the lack of event occurring in some mice. I don't think the binary approach would be as useful since only 10% of our mice never reach the event and there are several that have a long delay before the event. Does anyone have any advice? Thank you guys for your help.

From u2c7106159c8e6d5b at groups.google.com  Tue Mar  5 22:30:54 2019
From: u2c7106159c8e6d5b at groups.google.com (David Katz)
Date: Tue, 05 Mar 2019 22:30:54 +0000
Subject: Status of DOQTL genome scan model options
Message-ID: <QP2ryM3ysCc.NAY42nzQBAAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl2-disc/c/QP2ryM3ysCc/m/NAY42nzQBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

So, in DOQTL one had the option to choose between fitting a full model (on the 36 possible diplotype states) or an additive haplotype model (estimating effects for founder alleles). I've been looking through the user guide and can't find any information on whether both options remain available in qtl2 (and if so, how to select one vs. the other). Thanks for any help.

David

From u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com  Tue Mar  5 23:19:48 2019
From: u96353e7106735838 at groups.google.com (Karl Broman)
Date: Tue, 05 Mar 2019 23:19:48 +0000
Subject: Re: Status of DOQTL genome scan model options
Message-ID: <QP2ryM3ysCc.K1ufSoUXCgAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <QP2ryM3ysCc.NAY42nzQBAAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <QP2ryM3ysCc.NAY42nzQBAAJ@rqtl2-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl2-disc/c/QP2ryM3ysCc/m/K1ufSoUXCgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

If you call scan1() using the 36-state probabilities as output by calc_genoprob(), you'll be fitting the full model.

If you first convert the 36-state probabilities to allele dosages, using genoprob_to_alleleprob(), and then use those 8-state probabilities in scan1(), you'll be fitting the additive allele model.

karl

